<ClinicNote slug="dry-needling">
  <p>Dry needling uses very fine, single-use needles placed into tight or tender spots in muscle, often called trigger points. "Dry" means nothing is injected. It's different from acupuncture, which is based on traditional Chinese medicine; dry needling is guided by anatomy and your examination.</p>
  <h2>Does it work?</h2>
  <p>A 2023 umbrella review pulled together the systematic reviews on dry needling for musculoskeletal pain across the body:</p>
  <blockquote><p>Dry needling was "superior to sham/no intervention and equally effective to other interventions for pain reduction" in the short term.</p><cite>Chys et al., Journal of Clinical Medicine, 2023 [1]</cite></blockquote>
  <p>The same authors are clear about the limits:</p>
  <blockquote><p>"Rigorous evidence about the clinical effectiveness of this technique is still lacking."</p><cite>Chys et al., 2023 [1]</cite></blockquote>
  <h2>How we use it</h2>
  <p>Dry needling can ease pain and tightness so you can move and load the area more comfortably. That window is where the real work happens. At Zone Two it's always paired with exercise and a plan, and it's optional. If needles aren't for you, there are other ways to get the same job done.</p>
  <h2>Is it safe?</h2>
  <p>Side effects are usually mild and short-lived. Muscle soreness is the most common, along with occasional bruising, tiredness or a headache [2]. We'll go through your health history first, explain what to expect and ask for your consent before every session.</p>

